"A data lake zone diagram separates datasets by their processing guarantees and permitted promotion paths.\n\nUse when: Explain raw, validated, and consumption-ready data responsibilities. Review quality gates and ownership between storage zones.\n\nAvoid when: A single transformation expression or physical file layout is needed.\n\nVisual grammar: Padded zones denote stages with explicit data contracts. Dataset nodes identify retained outputs within each zone. Promotion arrows name transformations and validation conditions.\n\nBuild: Define the guarantees of each zone before naming it. Place representative datasets in the zone whose guarantees they meet. Add promotion, quarantine, and reprocessing paths. Keep zone headers clear and run cross-zone connectors through designated gaps.\n\nCheck: Zone names correspond to documented guarantees rather than implied universal standards. Raw retention and curated derivations remain distinguishable. Rejected or incomplete data cannot appear silently promoted. Zone borders never pass through dataset or connector labels.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming a bronze, silver, or gold label proves data quality. Using the diagram to imply all pipelines must have exactly three layers.\n\nExample: Raw Events are retained unchanged, validated Events remove malformed records, and Daily Sales exposes an agreed analytical grain.\n\nAlternatives: ETL pipeline diagram, Data lineage diagram."
